Erroneous answers pose the risk of **hallucination and factual inaccuracy**, potentially misleading users or degrading system trustworthiness. Even when faithfulness is ensured (e.g., via high-confidence retrieval or factuality scoring), errors can still occur because:  
- **Retrieval may be incorrect or noisy** — e.g., retrieved documents contain irrelevant or conflicting information, which—even if confidently scored—can mislead the LM (as shown in “When Not to Trust LMs”);  
- **Faithfulness metrics do not guarantee correctness** — they assess consistency *with retrieved content*, not truthfulness *against ground truth*; if the retrieved content itself is false or incomplete, faithful generation reproduces that error;  
- **Threshold-based decisions (Correct/Incorrect/Ambiguous) are imperfect** — ambiguous or borderline cases may retain low-quality knowledge, and “Correct”-labeled retrievals still contain “noisy knowledge strips” requiring refinement (per “Corrective RAG”).  

Thus, faithfulness ensures internal consistency, not external accuracy — erroneous answers persist when inputs (retrieved passages or model priors) are flawed.

Erroneous answers pose the risk of **hallucination and factual inaccuracy**, potentially misleading users or degrading system trustworthiness. Even when faithfulness is ensured (e.g., via high-confidence retrieval or factuality scoring), errors can still occur because:  
- **Retrieval may be incorrect or noisy** — e.g., irrelevant or misleading documents retrieved (as shown in “When Not to Trust LMs”, where 10% of questions worsen with retrieval due to low recall@1);  
- **Faithfulness metrics do not guarantee correctness** — they assess consistency *with retrieved content*, not truthfulness *against ground truth*; if the retrieved knowledge itself is false or incomplete, faithful generation propagates that error;  
- **Confidence thresholds are imperfect** — as in CRAG’s “Correct”/“Incorrect”/“Ambiguous” classification, borderline cases may mislead the system despite calibrated confidence scores.  

Thus, faithfulness ensures internal consistency, not external accuracy — erroneous answers persist when inputs (retrieved passages or model priors) are flawed.

The authors’ designed evaluation framework addresses limitations of traditional agent-based frameworks by moving beyond static, single-model or single-round evaluations—prone to randomness and bias—toward **robust, multi-perspective, and interactive evaluation strategies**. Specifically, it employs:  
- **Multi-model peer review** (e.g., CPAD, EvalMORAAL, decentralized peer review) to mitigate individual LLM evaluator biases;  
- **Conflict detection and majority voting** to enhance reliability;  
- **Cascaded selective evaluation**, dynamically routing evaluations by confidence to balance cost and accuracy; and  
- **Crowd-based comparative evaluation**, leveraging diverse LLM-generated judgments to capture richer, more nuanced assessments.  

These approaches collectively improve **stability, fairness, scalability, and contextual adaptability**—key shortcomings of traditional frameworks that often lack rigorous value alignment, continuous evolution assessment, or robust handling of uncertainty and harm.

